<p>Oil markets swung sharply as traders tried to price in conflicting signals about the Iran war—and the risk of disruption around the Strait of Hormuz, a key chokepoint for global crude shipments. Reports of an “effective closure” of the strait added to the uncertainty, lifting prices and volatility and quickly feeding into transport and other costs. Beyond the immediate shock, coverage said the conflict is reopening a bigger question: how to make energy systems more resilient. One argument is that efficiency gains and more domestically produced renewable power can, over time, reduce exposure to swings in imported fuel prices. The Financial Times cast those lessons as relevant long after the current fighting, as governments and companies rethink how to withstand the next geopolitical disruption.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ikro26D7O6fjAQcQ8vxbhzwd0Nlc_6oTBtdajbwldb7TvID26UEafUr82tQiSWkKnO-GLZ-UJxc67fSzApPrKAq9RSZPpO7zDOKdj7WojOfOpJyvSEi8B1Bzlo00he6KLGPz89DPCOchTaVes-pinDF9qCaFzekhnDSP9TwYkCDuzpSqYNY86x3hHU4U4eLMJENrwWE9KR1JGpF2elzR02-DefMiCRSrfMZLYk0xKwsi' alt='Fuel and energy supply concerns spread globally as the Iran war disrupts trade routes.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Hormuz bottleneck: The Strait of Hormuz carries about one-fifth of the world’s oil, so even a partial disruption can amplify price moves well beyond the Gulf region.</li><li>Intraday reversals: Al Jazeera reported crude prices swung from early gains to sharp drops as traders chased fast-changing headlines about the war and shipping through Hormuz.</li><li>Structural insulation: Experts quoted by DW said countries with more homegrown renewable electricity—and less reliance on imported oil and gas—tend to feel fewer knock-on effects from global price spikes, even if they can’t fully avoid them.</li><li>Stress-test takeaway: The Financial Times framed the war as a real-world stress test, showing where energy systems lack backup options and strengthening the case for planning that keeps both supply and demand flexible during future shocks.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Energy market analysts (PBS): PBS described the “effective closure” of the Strait of Hormuz as a major driver of the latest global economic shock from higher oil prices. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/war-with-iran-delivers-high-oil-prices-and-another-shock-to-the-global-economy'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>Energy experts (DW): Experts cited by DW said scaling homegrown renewables is the most reliable way to protect economies from future disruptions in oil-and-gas trade. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/iran-war-sends-oil-prices-climbing-these-countries-are-better-protected-thanks-to-renewables/a-76294122'>DW</a>)</li><li>Financial Times analysis: The FT stressed the longer-term economic fallout and framed the conflict as a catalyst for tougher, more durable energy-resilience policies. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/dab7d625-77f8-40ff-aeb9-451f81772125'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>Al Jazeera reporting: Al Jazeera highlighted how “mixed messages” about the war are fueling uncertainty and pushing oil markets into unusually sharp swings. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/3/11/oil-prices-swing-wildly-amid-mixed-messages-over-iran-war'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/3/11/oil-prices-swing-wildly-amid-mixed-messages-over-iran-war'>Oil prices swing wildly amid mixed messages over Iran war</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/dab7d625-77f8-40ff-aeb9-451f81772125'>The economic consequences of war with Iran</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/iran-war-sends-oil-prices-climbing-these-countries-are-better-protected-thanks-to-renewables/a-76294122'>Iran war sends oil prices climbing. These countries are better protected thanks to renewables</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/war-with-iran-delivers-high-oil-prices-and-another-shock-to-the-global-economy'>War with Iran delivers high oil prices and another shock to the global economy</a> - pbs.org</li></ul>